module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_control_value_reverted_twice_because_its_cost_was_never_priced

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable, StructurallyGuaranteed }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// THE CLASS: a control value is carried by its NUMBER ALONE, so the same ask can be answered both ways on
// the same day and nothing in the model records that a price was paid, or what it was. Found on srv3/srv4's
// GPU power caps, 2026-11-24; the class is any control whose value has a measured consequence and a purpose
// outside itself.
data a_control_value_reverted_twice_because_its_cost_was_never_priced: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_control_value_reverted_twice_because_its_cost_was_never_priced" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: MechanicallyPreventable },
      ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "reach the device write through a priced actuation only: converge_gpu_cap_over takes the PricedActuation instead of a bare Watt, and BootCapCommand carries the -lgc SM clock ceiling and the CPU scaling_max_freq cap it writes, so the apply-and-read-back fold cannot be handed a value that was never priced -- which also drops the last arm that can actuate a value with no consequence beside it (NoDemonstratedConstraint) for every control a unit actually writes, once the strip's rating plate is read onto a CircuitRating row, a per-member draw bound is stated, and one power.draw sample set sits beside each decode window",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: gunbc.serving.gpu_power_state_policy GpuPowerCapSelection had ONE arm, OperatorDeclaredCap, whose whole payload was a Watt plus who said it and when, so the only gate a cap ever passed asked whether the driver accepts the number (admit_gpu_cap_against joins the device's own reported [min, max]) and 30 W on an RTX 2000 Ada passes that gate forever, because 30 W IS the device's minimum settable limit (gunbc.fleet.gpu_device_observation power_limit_minimum). The producer-to-consumer slice -- ruling, admission, gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_converge writing and reading the value back, gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state keeping it across a reboot -- carried the value and never its consequence, and carried nothing at all for the electrical constraint the watts were being saved for. EARLIEST UNJUSTIFIED BOUNDARY: the value's first appearance as a bare Watt beside a name. The fleet's control set on 2026-11-24 was THREE values of exactly that shape -- the GPU power limit, the CPU frequency cap (scaling_max_freq, which no modeled converge writes) and the GPU SM clock ceiling (nvidia-smi -lgc, likewise) -- and none of them carried a consequence into the model.",

    "HARM: on 2026-11-24 the same 10 W per card was judged and re-judged in opposite directions on one day. srv3/srv4 went 40 W -> 30 W; at 30 W the SM clocks collapsed to 210-450 MHz under load and srv4's single-stream decode fell from 39.0 to 24.1 tok/s (engine journal lines 'done: N tokens in T s (R tok/s)', read over ssh by the operator's session -- a session read, not a pinned receipt); the operator judged that not worth 10 W per card and restored 40 W; then the surge strip feeding h11 + srv3 + srv4 opened a second time (~21:57 UTC; the first opening ~09:19 UTC at an estimated 1.3-1.6 kW, likely trigger h11's R9700 drawing 400-450 W in two-second spikes) and the caps went back to 30 W, with a 2.0 GHz CPU cap added on srv1-srv4. Both reversals were sound judgments made without a priced consequence beside the number, which is why both could be made at all.",

    "THE CAUSE WAS FOUND BY PRICING IT, NOT BY OPINION: the windows separate the actuators. 39.0 tok/s at 40 W / 3.0 GHz, 24.1 at 30 W / 3.0 GHz, 26.5 at 30 W / 2.0 GHz -- so the GPU power cap cost the throughput and the CPU frequency cap did not; releasing the CPU cap at a held 30 W made decode WORSE. srv4's CPU draw under the frequency cap read ~101 W -> ~40 W and srv3's ~65 W -> ~30 W (single hwmon apm_xgene readings taken while the load was also changing, so the operator's own note is that the true saving is somewhat smaller). The model could not state any of this while a candidate was 'the GPU watt value': the second comparison requires a candidate that carries both actuators.",

    "THE CLASS REPEATED ITSELF THE SAME EVENING, ON A SECOND CONTROL. At 22:45 UTC a locked-clock sweep (clocksweep.sh / kensweep.sh, 150 s per level, nvidia-smi power.draw and clocks.sm read once a second) settled on srv4 30 W with a 210-1500 MHz SM ceiling, srv3 40 W with no ceiling, and ken's T10s at 100 W with 300-1000 MHz. A clock ceiling is the same shape of choice as a power cap -- a control value, a measured consequence, a purpose -- and the sweep's own columns show what pricing it buys: at srv4's 30 W the unlocked level averaged 869 MHz at 29.1 W for 28.0 tok/s while 1500 MHz averaged 906 MHz at 27.0 W for 26.1 tok/s, and the 1800 MHz level read a HIGHER average clock, FEWER watts AND a higher decode median (888 MHz, 27.6 W, 31.9 tok/s) than the level chosen. The record says the choice was made on average clock and power because decode per 150 s window was noisy -- a decision taken without the two axes beside it, which is this row's defect wearing a different control. And the CARD conditions the reading: ken's two cool T10s fell 92-96 W -> 68-80 W at 1000 MHz while GPU1 at 82 C drew 83 W at EVERY level including 600-800 MHz, so '1000 MHz saves 15-25 W per card' is true of a card state, not of a control, and on a 3-way layer split the leaky card paces all three. gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing carries all three controls on one candidate and refuses a comparison across hosts or across known-different card states.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: gunbc.plans.gpu_card_host_program, which was to have run the N7 power sweep whose output std.decision would have selected over, was deleted 2026-11-08 and its sweep never landed, so the selection arm gpu_power_state_policy's own header says 'does not exist' had no producer -- while the declared arm stayed total. gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_power srv3_power/srv4_power are InferredFromSimilarBuild, so the cost side of the trade had no measured row either; the pricing fold therefore returns read obligations on that axis rather than an inferred draw. gunbc.plans.host_thermal_operating_point row T3 names ReadCpuOperatingLimits (scaling_max_freq and governor per core) as a fixed diagnostic that does not exist, which is why the CPU cap is priced here but not realized.",

    "NOT THE SAME CLASS AS gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_cap_set_here_surfaces_as_degradation_there: that row is a cap set in one place surfacing as degradation in another (cgroup shares against the 429 path), i.e. two authorities over one limit. This row is one limit carried with no price, so the same ask could be answered both ways. A cap could trip both rows, and the repairs differ: that one needs one authority, this one needs the consequence beside the value.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable -- a session could and did read tok/s and nvidia-smi clock frequencies by hand, and the driver refused values outside the device's range, which is mitigation by attention. CEILING: structurally guaranteed -- an unpriced value could not be expressed on the actuation path at all, so no cap could exist without its ask and its consequence. RUNG NOW: mechanically preventable on every path the model carries -- the converge refuses for any host, and a declared boot unit refuses on the host it boots; a unit that does not exist cannot be gated, and PresenceUnread is not the same as no unit, so the boot channel of the strip members is declared frontier below, not a covered path. In the fold, gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing has no carrier for the bad state (PowerAsk::AskUnstated has no circuit to forget and returns RealizationSelectionRefused; CircuitRating::RatingUnread and MemberDrawBound::BoundUnstated return SelectionNeedsEvidence naming what to read; DecodeReading::DecodeUnread and DrawReading::DrawUnread have no field for a rate, and std.pareto's feasibility scan refuses to compare a candidate whose funded-axis reading is absent; DrawReading::DrawRead carries a DrawSubject, so an nvidia-smi power.draw reading -- one subsystem, the card -- has no carrier onto the whole-host axis a circuit's per-member bound is stated against, a field without a feed-level reading cannot have that bound applied at all and is answered with the read obligation (the wattmeter), and a candidate on a machine the circuit does not carry is filtered out before selection; DrawReading::DrawRead carries a typed ThrottleShare -- the measured share of the window spent power-capped, in Permille -- and priced_draw_step refuses to price the watt step between two levels unless both sides' shares are read, so the fraction that tells a ceiling doing work from a freewheeling card changes the answer instead of decorating the row; and MemberDrawBound::BoundStated beside a RatingCited is refused, typed and located, when member count x member bound exceeds the cited carry limit -- a circuit opens on the SUM of its members and a per-member bound guarantees that sum only while the product fits, so the aggregate constraint can no longer be carried as if it were a local one), pinned by test.claim.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing_witness from both sides. On the actuation path, gunbc.fleet.gpu_cap_priced_actuation is the only road to a device write: its admitting arm is PricedActuation, whose fields are a candidate, the circuit the ask served, and the watt value the converge writes being candidate_cap of the candidate the admitting arm itself carries, so gpu_power_cap_converge writes what was weighed and gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state's census refuses by name an installed unit whose own cap line was never priced -- the refuse fails the boot converge run and keeps the units that Require it from starting; the model renders no unit files, so deriving the unit's own line from the priced candidate is the next rung, not a claim of this one, which is why gating the admission alone would have been theatre. Pinned by test.claim.fleet.gpu_cap_priced_actuation_witness, whose ports fixture succeeds at every write and reads back exactly what was asked, so a refused line there can only be the gate. The arm that does not yet refuse, NoDemonstratedConstraint, is derived rather than chosen: it admits only where no modeled circuit carrying the host has opened or stated a per-member bound (srv1 and ken today), and it prints the warrant saying so; reading a bound onto its breaker moves that host under the obligation, pinned by the same witness. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER (climbable now, unbuilt): the fold's signature carries the PricedActuation instead of a bare Watt and BootCapCommand carries its -lgc ceiling and scaling_max_freq, so no call site can reach the apply-and-read-back fold with an unpriced value.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_state_policy", decl_name: "GpuPowerCapSelection"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "price_power_cap_ask"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "CapCandidate"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.fleet_power_circuit", decl_name: "SharedPowerCircuit"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "priced_step"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "SmClockCeiling"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "CardCondition"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "DrawSubject"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "host_draw_interval"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "host_bound_evidence_gap"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "oversubscription_defects"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "ThrottleShare"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.gpu_power_cap_pricing", decl_name: "share_is_read"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_cap_priced_actuation", decl_name: "PricedActuation"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_cap_priced_actuation", decl_name: "ActuationAdmission"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_cap_priced_actuation", decl_name: "price_the_actuation"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_converge", decl_name: "gpu_cap_priced_lines_over"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state", decl_name: "boot_unit_restores_unpriced_cap"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_power_cap_boot_state", decl_name: "boot_unit_admits_every_command"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.fleet.gpu_cap_priced_actuation", decl_name: "no_constraint_warrant"),
  ],
}
